UPDATE companies SET description='Technical & Assembly Services Corporation (TASC) was founded in 1991 by Richard Hirst, a U.S. Air Force veteran, in Seattle, Washington. The veteran-owned company began with Hirst’s background spanning consumer electronics—such as electronic chess games and automated apple-labeling machines—to mission-critical systems including the emergency oxygen mask lighting for the Boeing 777.

TASC provides electronics manufacturing and assembly services including printed circuit board assembly, cable and wire harness assembly, full box builds, prototypes, design and development, testing, troubleshooting, repair, rework, transformer and coil winding, potting, and conformal coating. The shop runs flexible production lines capable of high-volume, low-volume, and single-unit runs from its Seattle facility, serving clients nationwide.

Customers range from OEMs and Fortune 500 firms to aerospace, medical equipment, U.S. military, government agencies, universities, and independent inventors. Past projects include components for the U.S. Navy, Department of Defense, Homeland Security applications, and a specialized computer-interconnect device used on the M1A1 Abrams tank.

The company emphasizes domestic production—“Proudly Made in the USA”—and a quality philosophy of “built in, not inspected in,” backed by ISO 9001 compliance, IPC Class II/III standards, ITAR registration, and proprietary process controls. An extended specialist network fills gaps for highly specialized processes.', certifications=ARRAY['ISO 9001 Compliant','IPC-A-610/620 Class II and III builds (latest revision)','IPC/WHMA-A-610/620E Rev CL I','RoHS compliant','ITAR Registered','UL certifiable (via client interface)','Electrostatic Discharge (ESD) Protection','NIST security standards (working toward certification)']::text[], profile='{"capabilities": ["Printed Circuit Board (PCB) Assembly", "Cable & Wire Harness Assembly", "Full Electronic Equipment Box Builds", "Prototypes and Demo Units", "Design and Development", "Testing", "Troubleshooting, Repair, Rework", "Transformer, Toroid, and Coil Winding", "Potting and Conformal Coating", "Materials Management and Parts Sourcing", "Reverse Engineering for Legacy Equipment", "Working with Incomplete Documentation"], "industries": ["Aerospace", "Medical equipment", "U.S. military", "Government agencies", "Government contractors", "Universities", "Entrepreneurs", "Inventors", "Designers", "OEMs", "Fortune 500", "Homeland Security", "U.S. Navy", "Department of Defense"], "materials": [], "equipment": [], "volume": "High-volume, low-volume, and 1-up runs", "lead_time": "", "tolerances": "", "built_on": ["Quality built in, not inspected in", "Proprietary techniques and quality control methodologies for quick turnarounds", "Domestic design, development, and manufacturing (Made in USA)", "Expedited assembly services: consignment, turnkey, quick turn", "Extended specialist network for highly specialized processes"], "facts_used": ["Founded 1991 by Richard Hirst (US Air Force veteran) -> /about", "Veteran-owned company -> /about", "Headquarters in Seattle, Washington -> /about", "Services: PCB Assembly, Cable & Wire Harness Assemb